: The patient appears to be wholely consumed by a new syndrome."pHer pupils have dilated, and she appears to need to lean forward at all times as if pulled in by something."~The patient reports single-minded purposeness-- a proclivity to dedicate herself entirely to and be possessed of a given task."�Most regrettably, the specifics of said task seem to be out of her control-- or else this affliction might have been a fortuitous development for productivity."FLORINIA: ...Indeed?"5The patient has been diagnosed with "Passion"..."A double-edged sword."�On one hand, even whimsically applied to the random subjects which this "Passion" seems to append itself to, it can prove a useful tool in many circumstances."�Statistics indicate that following "Passion" may result in an overwhelmingly significant number of benefits, ranging from production to personal health."_However, it would be more ideal were a different individual afflicted with this "Passion"."JFor this subject in particular to have developed it is a severe risk."1We shall consider the safest way to proceed."!-I have achieved confusion!-"`-She finally unlocked some positive emotions, but she doesn't seem happy about it at all!?-"-How could this be!?-"-You're almost there!-"-Give her the final push!-",FLORINIA: The logical fallacy inherent:"lDue to the subject's "Fear", she decided to repress various other emotional afflictions within herself."[However, the presence of "Fear" in and of itself belies one more, deeper, base desire."�An effective mechanism for survival, to be sure-- but where emotions are all, themselves, tools for survival, the suppression of them remains veritably maladaptive."�It is thus reasoned that the priority granted to "Fear" which permitted it to supercede all others and thus dissociate them from the subject must not have been a simple matter of survival."IGiven the circumstances, perhaps a social pressure or need instead--"But that would be..."FLORINIA: ..."
